			The rvtt Tensix backend
			=======================

This directory implements the Tensix vector (SFPU) extensions to the RISC-V
backend: 54 target passes, 113 -mtt- options, ~104k lines across 152 files.
It is large, so this file is the map.  Read it before the sources.

Machine descriptions here are named rvtt-*.md and are ordinary GCC .md files
reached from ../riscv.md -- "*.md" in this directory means machine
description, never Markdown.  Prose documentation lives in <repo>/docs/.


1. What the passes are for
--------------------------

The Tensix SFPU is a 32-lane vector unit with a small architectural register
file (8 LREGs), a tile-addressed destination file (Dst) reached through
implicit read/write counters (RWC), and two mechanisms for amortising
instruction delivery: replay (record a short sequence once, re-launch it
cheaply) and SFPLOADMACRO (a descriptor-driven macro engine).  A RISC-pushed
operation costs about 1.23x a replayed slot on the audited model, so most of
the optimisation effort is about delivering fewer words, not doing less work.

Standing design rule: no pass may recognise an operation by name, by a
coefficient fingerprint, or by a magic instruction word.  A transform that
cannot be derived from typed program structure must refuse -- by name, with
a recorded reason (see section 5).


2. Pass families
----------------

  macro planner (24 files)   SFPLOADMACRO descriptor synthesis, verification
			     and region scheduling against a resource
			     reservation table.  rtl-rvtt-macro-planner.cc
			     plus rvtt-macro-{desc,sched,epoch,tables,region,
			     ownership,verify}.
  scheduling (15 files)      Post-RA list scheduling over a typed-effect DAG,
			     hazard interlock filling, drain placement, and
			     Rau-style iterative modulo scheduling.
  replay formation (6)       Record/playback formation: hoisting a record out
			     of a loop, first-trip peel, outward lift across
			     enclosing loops, window sizing.
  LREG pressure (5)          DSATUR graph-colouring allocation over the 8
			     LREGs with spill-through-scratch, plus pressure
			     scheduling and renaming tiers.
  prgm constants (5)         Constant-register promotion and the priced
			     residency walk that keeps invariants resident.
  LUT selection (5)          If-conversion of piecewise range dispatch into
			     SFPLUTFP32 table selection, including the FP16
			     six-entry form.
  Dst / RWC (4)              Auto-increment addressing formation, per-row
			     address recurrence fusion, and store-to-load
			     forwarding over Dst.
  MOP (4)                    Hardware zero-overhead loop formation.
  crosscall (4)              Interprocedural LICM across noinline boundaries.
  crosslane (2)              Cross-lane permute and face-transpose surfaces.
  effects (2)                rvtt-effects.h is the single source of truth for
			     instruction effect classification.  New code must
			     query it rather than write another switch.  It
			     also holds the builtin-call helpers the passes
			     used to copy between themselves (argument
			     readers, id predicates); add a shared helper
			     there rather than a seventh private copy.

Supporting infrastructure that is not a pass: rvtt-cost / rvtt-delivery-cost
/ rvtt-timing / rvtt-trips (pricing engines), rvtt-bnb and rvtt-lpsolve (an
exact branch-and-bound solver used where a heuristic would guess), and six
opt-in host-compiled unit harnesses wired up in t-riscv-tt.


3. Where passes run
-------------------

Registrations live in rvtt-passes.def.  They cluster on a few anchors:

	BEFORE pass_expand		28
	BEFORE pass_all_optimizations	 8
	BEFORE pass_postreload		 5
	BEFORE pass_ira			 4
	AFTER  pass_postreload		 4
	BEFORE pass_free_cfg		 2
	one each: pass_lower_subreg, pass_complete_unroll,
		  AFTER pass_cse, AFTER pass_late_combine

IMPORTANT: within a single anchor, ordering is encoded by textual position in
rvtt-passes.def, and INSERT_PASS_AFTER reverses it -- each later line on the
same anchor lands closer to the anchor and therefore runs earlier.  The .def
file calls this out at the affected sites.  This is the least obvious thing
in the directory; check it before assuming an ordering.


4. Flags: what is on, what is not
---------------------------------

113 -mtt- options are declared in ../riscv.opt:

	Init(1)	   6	on by default
	Init(0)	  95	off by default
	Init(-1)  11	tri-state: cpu-derived errata workarounds, plus
			retirement tombstones that error() on use
	Init(32)   1	a numeric budget

Six enabled is what the built compiler actually reports, not just what the
source declares -- `riscv-tt-elf-g++ -Q --help=target` on the pin-59 install
prints 6 [enabled] and 93 [disabled].  Those six are:

	-mtt-tensix-optimize-cc
	-mtt-tensix-optimize-dce
	-mtt-tensix-optimize-dst-ownership
	-mtt-tensix-optimize-lut-select
	-mtt-tensix-optimize-replay
	-mtt-tensix-optimize-setexp-fold

HOW TO ENABLE THE REST.  Everything else is opt-in on the command line:

	riscv-tt-elf-g++ -mtt-tensix-optimize-<name> ...

and can be turned back off with the -mno- spelling.  Check what a build is
actually using rather than assuming:

	riscv-tt-elf-g++ -Q --help=target | grep '^ *-mtt-'

A flag being present is not a claim that it is production-recommended.  The
95 default-off options are a mix of reviewed optimisations, experimental
knobs and measurement scaffolding; four are testsuite fault-injection only
and are marked Undocumented.  To see what a pass did or why it declined, add
-fdump-<rtl|tree>-<pass name> and read the refusal (section 5).

CAVEAT ON "ON-39".  The campaign's ceremony records describe a reviewed
"ON set" of 39 flags.  That is NOT this compiler's default set -- the binary
enables six.  The ON-39 set is harness-side bookkeeping in tt-metal's
sweep_2x2.conf, and the committed material does not spell out the mapping
between the two.  If you need to reproduce the campaign's measured
configuration, take the flag list from the harness, not from this section.

No pass is enabled by belief.  A new pass lands compiled-in but off, and the
corpus gate proves that with the flag off every emitted binary is bit-for-bit
identical to the previous build.  Promotion into the default set requires
dump-proven evidence that the flag transforms code in the installed binary,
the full delta adjudicated program by program, a bit-accuracy gate, and
re-measurement of every touched row.


5. Refusals
-----------

rvtt-refusals.def is a registry of named reasons a transform declined.  Every
refusal a pass can emit is registered there; t-riscv-tt regenerates
rvtt-refusals.txt from it and fails the build on a duplicate or unregistered
name.  "It did not fire" is therefore never silent -- there is always a name.

The registry is append-only by rule, and the testsuite pins dump text, so
removing a name can break a test that asserts the exact string.  Note also
that some names are composed at run time (rvtt_refusal_fire_composed), so a
name absent from a grep of the sources is not necessarily dead.


6. Vocabulary
-------------

Terms used throughout the sources that are not standard GCC:

  Dst		 the destination tile register file, addressed in 16x16 faces
  RWC		 read/write counters -- implicit Dst address pointers advanced
		 by SETRWC/INCRWC, which passes must model to know which face
		 is live
  LREG		 one of the 8 architectural SFPU vector registers
  replay	 record a short instruction sequence once, re-launch cheaply
  MOP		 the Tensix zero-overhead hardware loop engine
  row		 one issue of a parameterised instruction sequence
  delivered word the unit the delivery cost model prices
  refusal	 a named, recorded decision not to transform (section 5)
  priced	 a decision taken by asking a cost engine, not a heuristic
  licensed	 an optimisation permitted to change numerical results, under
		 explicit sign-off and a machine-checked divergence certificate
  proof		 an entry under proofs/, wired into the build, backing a
		 transform's legality claim


7. Testing
----------

Tests are in ../../../testsuite/g++.target/riscv/tt/.  Most passes carry
dump-scan tests keyed to their dump name; the pre-campaign lowering passes are
covered by assembly scans instead.

proofs/ holds eleven machine-checked equivalence results.  Be precise about
what the build checks: only two of them -- store-sink-roundtrip and
stochrnd-store-round -- are verified by t-riscv-tt, which regenerates
rvtt-storefold-verdicts.def from their RESULT files and fails the build if the
checked-in table is not byte-identical.  Six more are cited from the sources as
the legality backing for a transform, and three (cast-fp16a-rne,
shft-imm-vs-reg, sm32-cast-elision-shift) are cited only from proofs/README.md
and the campaign notes that record the refusal they justify.  Nothing outside
those two is enforced mechanically.

Two kinds of host-compiled tooling live here and are NOT part of the compiler
build, so do not mistake them for dead code:

  *-test.cc		  six opt-in unit harnesses, wired up in t-riscv-tt
  rvtt-macro-vocab-enum.cc  an offline template-word vocabulary enumerator.
			  It is in no build rule by design; it documents its
			  own build line near the top of the file and seven
			  entries in rvtt-refusals.def cite it as the
			  provenance of a refusal reason.


8. Reading order for a newcomer
-------------------------------

  1. this file
  2. rvtt-passes.def -- the pipeline, with its ordering caveats
  3. rvtt-effects.h -- the effect vocabulary everything else should use
  4. <repo>/docs/MACRO_PLANNER.md and docs/LREG_ALLOCATOR.md for the two
     largest subsystems
  5. a small self-contained pass, e.g. gimple-rvtt-int-not.cc (295 lines),
     before one of the 3000-line ones
